# Material for apriltag_plate.obj.
#
# map_Kd resolves RELATIVE TO THIS FILE, so the PNG sits in this same meshes/
# directory rather than under materials/ — that keeps the texture out of
# Gazebo's resource-path resolution entirely, which is one less thing to get
# wrong.
#
# tag36h11_00000.png IS COMMITTED, next to this file. It is tag36h11 id 0 from
# AprilRobotics/apriltag-imgs — the family's own dataset — upscaled x100 with
# NEAREST-NEIGHBOUR filtering to 1000x1000 (4 KB).
#
# Do not redraw or re-export it. An AprilTag is not a drawable pattern: the
# cells encode a specific 36-bit codeword per id, so an invented pattern is not
# a tag and simply never decodes. To change id, take the matching PNG from that
# repo and upscale it the same way:
#
#   python3 -c "from PIL import Image; i=Image.open('tag36_11_00007.png').convert('RGB'); \
#               i.resize((1000,1000), Image.NEAREST).save('tag36h11_00007.png')"
#
# The upscale must be nearest-neighbour. A smoothing filter softens the cell
# edges, and soft edges cost detection RANGE — a quiet failure that looks like a
# camera or lighting problem.
#
# Ks 0 and a flat illum: an AprilTag is a matte printed sheet. A specular
# highlight across it is a real way to lose detections, in sim as on a robot.
newmtl tag
Ka 1.000 1.000 1.000
Kd 1.000 1.000 1.000
Ks 0.000 0.000 0.000
d 1.0
illum 1
map_Kd tag36h11_00000.png
